Zimbabwe has been an uncomfortable place  to live for much of the last two decades  residents of mapo-gu a township east of  Harare know this all too well access to  running water in their homes has been a  dream and have had to rely on boreholes  mostly provided by foreign donors it's  been like this for something like ten  years now we have to wake up early  around 4:00 a.m. or 3:00 a.m. for you to  get water like you see this kill you we  have to wake up early so that you get  enough water it's really painful and  it's really not a good life for us to do  so much of Zimbabwe's infrastructure  fell into decline during Robert Mugabe's  rule and the former president's failure  to deal with corruption and the economy  has had a devastating impact on the  country's public health sector it's very  difficult to get permission to film  inside a hospital here so I secretly  recorded this video on my cell phone  this is one of the biggest hospitals in  the country and it's astonishing to see  a facility relying on old equipment it's  largely under-resourced and its staff or  overwhelmed medical practitioners often  can't administer treatment because of a  lack of resources dr. Edgar Munez he  tells me it's hard to have to walk away  from a patient who can't afford health  care a medical bill of $10 can be too  much for some Zimbabweans is very much  heartbreaking and draining to look at  your patients when they are suffering  and sometimes you have to ask them if  they do have God so they're able to sell  a house or for them to be able to afford  the service that we are providing and  yet this is a public institution  sometimes you lose so many patients from  illness that is defeatable Zimbabwe was  once the breadbasket of southern Africa  boasting some of the best infrastructure  in the region today the vast majority of  people here have no jobs and the  country's infrastructure has been  crumbling for years many of the railway  networks are no longer in use  some roads haven't been serviced in  years with the persistent decline in  Zimbabwe's political and economic  situation millions have left the country  in search of greener pastures many of  them young and skilled further  impoverishing an already battered nation
 

